Q: Read large text file in java, infeasible? A: <h2>Modern Java</h2>
<p>Let's see what happens using the features of modern Java.</p>
<p>We use <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/nio/file/Files.html#readString(java.nio.file.Path)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Files.readString</code></a> to load the entire contents of the file into memory. We then display in a <code>JTextArea</code> in a <code>JFrame</code> laid out with <code>BorderLayout</code>. We have a button bar on top to display a file-picker. And that button bar also displays the elapsed time and size of file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>package work.basil.example.swing;

import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.time.Duration;

public class FileViewer extends JFrame
{
    void main ( )
    {
        IO.println( Runtime.version() );
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater( FileViewer :: new );
    }

    public FileViewer ( )
    {
        super( &quot;File Viewer&quot; );
        this.setDefaultCloseOperation( WindowConstants.EXIT_ON_CLOSE );
        this.setSize( 900 , 600 );
        this.setLayout( new BorderLayout() );

        final JTextArea textArea = new JTextArea();
        textArea.setEditable( false );
        this.add( new JScrollPane( textArea ) , BorderLayout.CENTER );

        final JButton openButton = new JButton( &quot;Open File…&quot; );
        JLabel pathLabel = new JLabel( &quot;No file selected&quot; );

        final JPanel topPanel = new JPanel(); // FlowLayout by default
        topPanel.add( openButton );
        topPanel.add( pathLabel );
        this.add( topPanel , BorderLayout.NORTH );

        openButton.addActionListener( e -&gt; {
            JFileChooser chooser = new JFileChooser();
            if ( chooser.showOpenDialog( this ) == JFileChooser.APPROVE_OPTION )
            {
                String size = null;
                Path path = chooser.getSelectedFile().toPath();
                long start = System.nanoTime();
                try
                {
                    size = formatSize( Files.size( path ) );
                    String content = Files.readString( path );
                    textArea.setText( content );
                    textArea.setCaretPosition( 0 );
                }
                catch ( IOException ex )
                {
                    JOptionPane.showMessageDialog( this , &quot;Error reading file: &quot; + ex.getMessage() );
                }
                Duration duration = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - start );
                Toolkit.getDefaultToolkit().beep();
                pathLabel.setText( size + &quot; ⟡ &quot; + duration + &quot; ⟡ &quot; + path.toString() );
            }
        } );

        setVisible( true );
    }

    // Optional: convert bytes to KB, MB, GB, etc.
    private static String formatSize ( long bytes )
    {
        if ( bytes &lt; 1024 ) return bytes + &quot; B&quot;;

        int unit = 1024;
        String[] units = { &quot;KB&quot; , &quot;MB&quot; , &quot;GB&quot; , &quot;TB&quot; };
        int exp = ( int ) ( Math.log( bytes ) / Math.log( unit ) );

        return String.format( &quot;%.2f %s&quot; ,
                bytes / Math.pow( unit , exp ) ,
                units[ exp - 1 ] );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/82Ri0QPT.jpg" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/82Ri0QPT.jpg" alt="screenshot of example app loading 96 meg file in less that two and a half seconds on Mac with Apple Silicon" /></a></p>
<p>An a MacBook Pro 16&quot; with Apple Silicon M1 Pro chip, running macOS 16.7.5, using Java 26, we see that loading a 96 meg file takes less that two and a half seconds.</p>
<h3>File I/O is <em>not</em> the bottleneck</h3>
<p>If we add code around the <code>Files.readString</code> line to record time elapsed while actually reading the file:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>                    final long startLoad = System.nanoTime();
                    String content = Files.readString( path );
                    IO.println( &quot;Loaded file in &quot; + Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - startLoad ) );
</code></pre>
<p>… we see that actually reading the file from SSD takes only a tenth of a second (PT0.107376083S). So nearly all of the 2+ seconds of our overall elapsed time is Swing, not file I/O.</p>
<p>You said:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>all I know is using BufferedReader is the best.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As we have seen in code above, file I/O is <em>not</em> the bottleneck. Nearly all the time is spent in Swing, not in file I/O.</p>
<h3>Workarounds</h3>
<p>As noted in the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/15731961/642706">Answer by wchargin</a>, you should not be calling <code>JTextArea#append</code> repeatedly for every line. That is not helping.</p>
<p>If you want to gradually load the file without locking up the user interface, you should be loading the file in chunks in the background. For example, use <code>Files.lines</code> while you check for every so many lines having been read. Then, for each of those chunks, post the additional text to the UI across threads using <code>SwingWorker</code>. <strong>Never</strong> access/manipulate the user-interface from a background thread! See Oracle tutorial, <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/uiswing/concurrency/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>Concurrency in Swing</em></a>. I do not know if this will really help much, since the file I/O is taking virtually no time.</p>
<p>Perhaps the best solution is to lock-up the user-interface while displaying a busy-working indicator such as a spinning wheel.</p>

Q: Best way to handle similar type of function without writing them again in C++ A: <p>You could move the repeated code into a helper function. If I understand the intent of this question, the &quot;calculations that only use 'var' variable&quot; are what you are concerned about repeating. So, use the standard strategy of moving repeated code into its own function. This helper would handle everything your original function does up to the point where <code>func</code> is invoked. (If there was common functionality after invoking <code>func</code>, that could be put into a second helper function; this is left up to the reader.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>double BigCalculation(double var) // Consider using a more descriptive name.
{
    // calculations omitted
    return var /*should be var2, but that calculation was omitted */;
}
</code></pre>
<p>At this point, writing a bunch of overloads does not involve so much repetition. (These could be simplified, but I'll keep the variable names for now to make it easier to compare to the code in the question.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>double BigFunction(double (*func)(double), double var)
{
    double var2 = BigCalculation(var);
    double var3 = func(var2);
    return var3;
}

double BigFunction(double (*func)(double, double *), double var, double *para)
{
    double var2 = BigCalculation(var);
    double var3 = func(var2, para);
    return var3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>While this is better, it still requires work for each function signature. A function template would let you combine your overloads into one definition. (For this version, I'll simplify away <code>var2</code>. If you also simplify away <code>var3</code>, this becomes a one-liner.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class... Args&gt;
double BigFunction(double (*func)(double, Args...), double var, Args... params) {
    double var3 = func(BigCalculation(var), params...);
    return var3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>One advantage of this approach is that while <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/495021">templates must be defined in a header</a>, the big calculation can be kept in a source file. If the calculation needs to be modified (as a bug fix?), only one translation unit would need to be re-compiled.</p>
<hr />
<p>Personally, I would be inclined to change the design so that this <code>BigCalculation</code> is what external code would call. It's less work for you, since the <code>BigFunction</code> template would be dropped, and the change to call sites is marginal. Compare calling your version</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>BigFunction(func, var, para)
</code></pre>
<p>to calling the calculation directly and manually invoking <code>func</code></p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>func(BigCalculation(var), para)
</code></pre>
<p>The syntax is similar, and there is no intrinsic reason to prefer one over the other.</p>
<p>(The situation would be different if there was common functionality <strong>after</strong> invoking <code>func</code> and before returning a value. In that case, there would be two helper functions to call, which adds value to the <code>BigFunction</code> wrapper.)</p>
